[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Patch the upstream tree-sitter grammar in an overlay rather than forking it

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-GRAMMAR-PATCH-NOT-FORK
TITLE: Patch the upstream tree-sitter grammar in an overlay rather than forking it
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
Upstream revives and the patch stops applying, or the patch set grows past what
is reviewable as a diff, or the fix lands upstream and the patch is deleted.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The sdoc tree-sitter grammar is consumed as an upstream source archive with a
small patch series applied in the overlay. No fork repository is created.

The patch targets the grammar definition, not the generated parser, so the
build must regenerate. A patched grammar definition with a stale generated
parser is a silent no-op: the build succeeds and the behavior is unchanged.

The grammar's own test corpus lives in this repository as a check, not in the
grammar source. Upstream ships none.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Patching the definition keeps the diff around thirty reviewable lines. Forking
would mean carrying a regenerated parser of roughly eighteen thousand lines in
version history, for a change that is three rules.

Upstream has been dormant for 336 days, so drift risk is low, and fuzz-free
patch application makes any drift a loud build failure rather than a quiet
misapplication.

If the fix is accepted upstream later, a fork created at that point is a contribution
vehicle rather than a dependency, and a merged fix deletes the patch instead of
leaving a stale fork to reconcile.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Two build details found by reading the nixpkgs grammar builder. Its generate
step runs a bare generate command after changing into the path declared in the
grammar metadata; this grammar declares no path and keeps its definition in a
subdirectory, so the step fails without an override. And the metadata declares
a version and a file-type list that both disagree with the repository state.
<<<
